        title: 'The Dishwasher Nobody Used',
        body: 'When the Ferguson family finally closed on the house in Decatur, Grandma Ida walked through it like a woman touring a palace. Four bedrooms. Two full baths. A kitchen with a dishwasher built right under the counter. Eleven-year-old Marcus loaded it that first Sunday after dinner, proud as a deacon. Monday morning he found every plate back in the rack beside the sink, washed by hand. Grandma Ida had pulled them all out. "I don’t trust that thing," she said. "Water’s too hot. It’ll crack my good dishes. Besides, I washed dishes this way for sixty years." For a month the dishwasher sat there, shining and empty, holding the dish towels. Marcus brought it up at youth night, laughing, and Minister Rolle, who was Bahamian and had heard Myles Munroe preach in Nassau when he was a boy, laughed with him and then turned serious. "Dr. Munroe used to tell about his family moving into a house with a brand-new bathtub," he said. "They kept on bathing the old way, in a tin tub, because that is what they knew. His point was that you can move a people into a new house and they can still carry the old one in their heads. Freedom is not only a new address. It is a new mind." That night Marcus did not argue with his grandmother. He asked her to show him how she washed her good dishes, and he listened. Then he asked if he could show her what the machine could do, just the everyday plates. She folded her arms, but she watched. On Saturday the plates came out hot and clean, and Grandma Ida held one up to the light for a long time. "Hm," she said. "Sixty years." The good china still gets washed by hand. Some things deserve it. But the dish towels moved to a drawer, and Grandma Ida now tells the ladies at church that she has a machine that works for her. Marcus wrote the verse Minister Rolle gave them on an index card and stuck it to the dishwasher door: "Stand fast therefore in the liberty wherewith Christ hath made us free" (Galatians 5:1).',
