        title: 'The Notebook in the Glovebox',
        body: 'On a Sunday night in November 2014, Pastor Andre Wells sat in his car outside Greater New Hope in Memphis and read the news on his phone: Dr. Myles Munroe, his wife Ruth and seven others, killed when their plane struck a crane on Grand Bahama. Andre had been nineteen when he heard Munroe in a packed arena talk about the cemetery being the richest place on earth, full of songs never sung and books never written. He had gone home that night and started a notebook: a plan for an after-school academy for the boys on his street, who had nowhere to go between the last bell and the streetlights. The notebook was still in his glovebox. He knew that without looking. He had moved it from car to car for sixteen years. There was always a reason. The building fund came first. Then the roof. Then his mother got sick. The academy stayed on paper. He sat in the dark lot until the security light clicked off. He thought about a man who had preached on four continents and still died in the middle of a trip, on the way to teach one more conference. Nobody gets to pick the day. In the morning he called two deacons and a retired schoolteacher named Sister Cooper, and he read them the first page of the notebook, his own handwriting at nineteen. Sister Cooper was quiet a long time. Then she said, "Baby, I have been praying for somebody to say that for ten years." The academy opened in the fellowship hall the next September with nine boys and three folding tables. It was not what the notebook drew. It was better, because it was real. Andre keeps the notebook on his desk now, open, and under it a card with the verse he preached the Sunday after the crash: "Moreover it is required in stewards, that a man be found faithful." (1 Corinthians 4:2). He tells the boys the same thing every fall. You do not own your gift. You were trusted with it. Do not take it to the grave.',
