        title: 'The Badge at Model UN',
        body: 'Kiara Jenkins had never worn a blazer to school before. But the Model United Nations conference at Morehouse was Saturday, and her team had drawn the nation of Ghana, and Ms. Adebayo said delegates dress like delegates. On Friday afternoon Ms. Adebayo handed out the laminated badges. "Read what it says," she told them. Kiara read it out loud: "Delegate of the Republic of Ghana." "Not Kiara," Ms. Adebayo said. "Ghana. Tomorrow when you stand up, you are not giving your opinion. You are giving your nation’s position. If you don’t know what Ghana thinks, you look it up before you open your mouth." Kiara laughed, but she spent Friday night reading Ghana’s real statements on climate and trade. Saturday she did fine. She even got a nod from the chair. Monday was harder. At lunch, Destiny started in on a girl from their church who had moved schools, saying things about her family that Kiara was pretty sure were not true, and the whole table was leaning in. Kiara opened her mouth to add the thing she had heard too. Then she felt the weight of a badge that was not there. Her pastor had preached on Sunday from a book by a Bahamian preacher, Myles Munroe, who said that when you meet an ambassador you are meeting a whole nation, and that an ambassador does not get to speak his personal opinion while he is on duty. Pastor had read Paul’s words twice: "Now then we are ambassadors for Christ" (2 Corinthians 5:20). Kiara closed her mouth. Then she opened it again. "I don’t think we know that," she said. "And if it was us, we wouldn’t want it said." The table got quiet. Destiny rolled her eyes. Somebody changed the subject to the game. It was not dramatic. Nobody clapped. But on the bus home Kiara texted the girl who had moved, just to say she missed her at youth group. She got a heart back, and three crying faces, and a long message she read twice. That night she asked her mom for an index card and wrote on it: Delegate of the Kingdom of God. She clipped it inside her binder where only she could see it.',
