        title: 'Forty Programs and One Question',
        body: 'Deacon Harold Pierce had served Mount Calvary on Chicago’s South Side for thirty-one years, and he could name every ministry in the bulletin from memory: the usher board, the pastor’s aid, the anniversary committee, the building fund, the Easter pageant, forty programs in all. He had chaired half of them. Then Darnell Haynes was buried. Darnell was nineteen and had grown up in the nursery of that church. He had stopped coming at fourteen. Nobody had noticed for a year. He died two blocks from the church at eleven at night, outside a store whose owner knew his name better than the church did. At the repast Deacon Pierce sat with a plate he did not eat and listened to Darnell’s aunt say, gently, that the boy had told her once the church was a nice building where old people had meetings. That night Harold could not sleep. He took down a book his son had given him years before, Rediscovering the Kingdom, and found the line his son had underlined: Munroe’s argument that religion can keep you so busy that it distracts you from your hunger for the Kingdom itself. He read Jesus’ words in Matthew 6 again: "But seek ye first the kingdom of God, and his righteousness; and all these things shall be added unto you." (Matthew 6:33). He counted the programs. He could not find the Kingdom in most of them. He found buildings, titles, schedules and money. He found very little of the King’s rule actually reaching the block. At the next deacons’ meeting he asked for ten minutes and used them to ask one question: which of our programs would Darnell have known about? Nobody answered. It took a year. Some programs were closed with thanks. The anniversary committee shrank by half. The men’s ministry started standing outside the store on Friday nights with hot chocolate and a folding table. The youth pastor started knowing names. Deacon Pierce does not claim it would have saved Darnell. He only says that the Kingdom is supposed to come to the block, and for a long time it had stayed inside the building.',
