        title: 'Whose Tomatoes',
        body: 'The vacant lot behind Bethel AME in South Dallas had been weeds and broken glass for nine years until Mother Lucille Barnes got tired of looking at it. She was eighty-one and still drove herself to Bible study, and she announced one Wednesday that the lot was going to be a garden by spring. By May there were twelve raised beds and a sign the youth painted: Bethel Kingdom Garden. Trouble came in July, with the tomatoes. Twelve-year-old Jaylen had planted bed number four, and his tomatoes came in first, fat and red, and he guarded them like a dog with a bone. When the Hendersons’ little girls picked two, he yelled loud enough for the pastor to come out of his office. Mother Barnes called Jaylen over to the bench under the pecan tree. "Whose lot is this?" she asked. "The church’s." "And whose is the church?" He knew this one. "Jesus’." "Whose dirt? Whose rain? Who made tomato seeds hold tomatoes?" Jaylen looked at his shoes. "Yahweh’s." "Mm-hm. So whose tomatoes?" He did not answer, but he was thinking. Mother Barnes told him about a preacher from the Bahamas she used to listen to on cassette, who said that in the Kingdom the wealth is common, and that the King owns everything because He made everything. "That doesn’t mean you don’t work your bed," she said. "You worked it good. It means you’re a manager, not an owner. Owners fight and hoard and guard. Managers work hard and share, because they know whose it is." She opened her Bible on her lap and had him read it out: "The earth is the LORD’s, and the fulness thereof; the world, and they that dwell therein." (Psalms 24:1). That Saturday Jaylen set up a card table at the gate with a hand-lettered sign: FREE. BED 4. TAKE WHAT YOU NEED. The Henderson girls came first. By August, half the beds had joined him, and old Mr. Tate down the block, who had not set foot in a church in twenty years, started coming by at seven every morning to water, and stayed for the eleven o’clock service by October.',
