        title: 'The Fire Escape Garden',
        body: 'Amara Okafor-Johnson was ten when her Sunday school teacher at Bethany Baptist in Harlem gave every child a paper cup of dirt with a basil seed in it and told them the Kingdom of Heaven was like a garden. Amara took hers home and set it on the fire escape outside the kitchen window, where the sun hit for three hours in the afternoon. It came up. Then her mother bought her two more cups, mint and a tomato plant, and her grandfather, who had grown yams in Nigeria before he came to New York, built her a wooden box out of a shipping pallet. By July the fire escape was green. Mrs. Rivera downstairs started asking for basil. The Haitian family on the fourth floor traded thyme for mint. Old Mr. Coleman across the hall, who never spoke to anybody, stopped one day on the stairs and said his mother used to grow collards in Alabama, and could Amara grow collards up there? She did not know. They tried it together. In August Amara’s teacher read the class a short line from a Bahamian preacher named Myles Munroe, who wrote that when Yahweh set out to bring Heaven to earth, He planted a garden, and that His Kingdom grows gradually and often invisibly. Then she read Jesus’ parable about the mustard seed: "It is like a grain of mustard seed, which a man took, and cast into his garden; and it grew, and waxed a great tree; and the fowls of the air lodged in the branches of it." (Luke 13:19). Amara raised her hand. "It’s like my fire escape," she said. "It was just one cup. Now the whole building comes." Her teacher smiled. "And what grew besides basil?" Amara thought about Mrs. Rivera, and the Haitian family, and Mr. Coleman, who had come to her birthday party with a card he had clearly bought at the drugstore and signed in careful old handwriting. "People talking to each other," she said. That fall the building’s tenants’ association voted to turn the rooftop into a garden. Mr. Coleman was elected to run it. He opens every meeting with a prayer now, short and a little shy, and he still calls Amara the boss.',
