        title: 'What the Governor Took',
        body: 'Grandma Eunice Rolle was born in Nassau in 1949 and moved to Miami in 1970, and she rarely talked about the old days. So when her granddaughter Simone came home from tenth grade with a family-history project, Eunice surprised everyone by talking for two hours. She told Simone about school mornings before independence, standing in rows in the heat and singing Rule Britannia to a queen none of them had ever seen. She told her about learning the dates of English kings by heart and never once hearing the name of a single African ruler. She told her how her own grandmother had spoken a few words of an African language she would not teach the children, because the teachers said it was backward. Then she was quiet. "They didn’t beat it out of us," she said. "They just taught us to be ashamed of it. That was worse." Simone did not know what to say. She had read the chapter her pastor assigned from Myles Munroe’s book, where he said the colonial governors took three things from his people: their language, their history and their culture. "Grandma," she said slowly, "do you think we can get them back?" Eunice took a long time to answer. Then she got up and brought the old family Bible from her bedroom. On the front pages were names going back five generations, the oldest written by a hand that could barely spell. "This is history the governor couldn’t take," she said. "Somebody wrote it down." She opened to the back, to Revelation, and had Simone read: "After this I beheld, and, lo, a great multitude, which no man could number, of all nations, and kindreds, and people, and tongues, stood before the throne, and before the Lamb, clothed with white robes, and palms in their hands;" (Revelation 7:9). "Every nation," Eunice said. "Every tongue. He’s not going to make us English in heaven, baby. He’s going to make us His. All of us, from everywhere, with our own faces." Simone’s project came back with an A. But what she kept was the recording on her phone of her grandmother praying in the kitchen afterward, in her Bahamian voice, plain and unashamed, the way nobody ever taught her in school.',
