        title: 'The Speech from the Throne',
        body: 'DeShawn Ferguson was fifteen and had recently discovered that saying "God told me" ended most arguments with his little sister. God told me it was my turn on the TV. God told me you should do the dishes. It worked until his grandfather heard it. Papa Cleveland was born in Nassau and had lived in Atlanta for forty years, and he still said "the Governor" the way some people say "the President." He sat DeShawn down on the back porch. "When I was a boy," he said, "the British governor lived in a big pink house on Duke Street. Once a year the whole town went down to the park by the water, and he sat in a red chair with a crown behind it, and he read us the Speech from the Throne. Words from the Queen. You know what he never did?" DeShawn shook his head. "He never once added his own. Not one sentence. If he had stood up there and said, the Queen says DeShawn gets the remote, they would have put him on the next boat to England." DeShawn laughed in spite of himself. Papa Cleveland opened his worn Bible to John 16 and made him read it out loud: "Howbeit when he, the Spirit of truth, is come, he will guide you into all truth: for he shall not speak of himself; but whatsoever he shall hear, that shall he speak: and he will shew you things to come." (John 16:13). "You see that? He shall not speak of himself. The Holy Ghost is the real Governor, and even He only says what He hears from the Father and the Son. Dr. Munroe used to preach that. So who are you to put words in the King’s mouth over a TV?" DeShawn did not have an answer. He also did not say "God told me" for a long time after that. What happened instead surprised him. He started reading the Gospel of John at night, a chapter at a time, mostly because he wanted to know what the King actually had said. Three weeks in, his sister asked why he was being so nice. He told her the truth: he was trying to find out what the real speech from the throne was before he quoted it again.',
