        tone: 'solemn',
        title: 'Who Is Tending Your Garden',
        body: 'Renee Holloway had been clean for eleven months when she found the plot. It was a weedy rectangle behind the recovery house in West Baltimore, and the house mother, Sister Gloria, said whoever wanted it could have it. Nobody else did. Renee spent April on her knees pulling out what had grown there unattended: thistles, broken bottles, a root system of something tough and grey that went down farther than she expected. It reminded her of things she did not want to remember. One Tuesday at Bible study Sister Gloria read from an old book by a Bahamian preacher who called the Holy Spirit the Master Gardener, the Governor who carries out the King’s will and turns earth into the likeness of Heaven. Then she read the line he asked his readers, and she asked it again, looking around the circle: who is tending your garden? Renee did not sleep much that night. For fifteen years something else had tended her garden. It had planted fear and shame and need, and she had watered it. Being clean was not the same as being tended. She had pulled weeds for eleven months. She had never once asked the Gardener in. The next morning she went out to the plot before anyone was awake and knelt in the dirt, and for the first time she did not pray for strength to keep going. She prayed for the Holy Spirit to take over the ground. She did not feel anything dramatic. But over that summer the women in the house noticed something. Renee was gentler. She stopped flinching when people raised their voices. She started laughing. In August Sister Gloria brought out her Bible at the picnic and read the fruit of the Spirit while Renee handed out tomatoes from the plot: "But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, gentleness, goodness, faith," (Galatians 5:22). Renee had the verse written on a stake at the end of the row. Under it, in marker, she had added three words: He tends it.',
